1
00:02:52,500 --> 00:02:58,500
This ride will be the death of you.

2
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:51,000
Final Destination 3 is a horror, mystery film directed by James Wong.

3
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:43,500
High school senior Wendy's premonition of a deadly rollercoaster ride saves her life and a lucky
few, but not from death itself — which seeks out those who escaped their fate.

4
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:36,000
Wendy Christensen, played by Mary Elizabeth Winstead.

5
00:14:22,500 --> 00:14:28,500
Kevin Fischer, played by Ryan Merriman.

6
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:21,000
Ian McKinley, played by Kris Lemche.

7
00:20:07,500 --> 00:20:13,500
Erin, played by Alexz Johnson.

8
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:06,000
Frankie Cheeks, played by Sam Easton.

9
00:25:52,500 --> 00:25:58,500
Jason Wise, played by Jesse Moss.

10
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:51,000
Final Destination 3 is a 2006 American supernatural horror film produced and
directed by James Wong, who co-wrote it with Glen Morgan.

11
00:31:37,500 --> 00:31:43,500
It is a standalone sequel to Final Destination 2 (2003) and the
third installment in the Final Destination film series.

12
00:34:30,000 --> 00:34:36,000
It stars Mary Elizabeth Winstead and Ryan Merriman, and
takes place six years after the first film.

13
00:37:22,500 --> 00:37:28,500
Winstead plays Wendy Christensen, a high school graduate who has a premonition
that a roller coaster she and her classmates are riding will derail.

14
00:40:15,000 --> 00:40:21,000
Although she saves some of them, Death begins hunting the survivors.

15
00:43:07,500 --> 00:43:13,500
Wendy realizes that photographs she took at the
attraction contain clues about the deaths.

16
00:46:00,000 --> 00:46:06,000
With survivor and friend Kevin Fischer (Merriman), Wendy tries to use this
knowledge to save the rest of the survivors and stop Death's scheme.

17
00:48:52,500 --> 00:48:58,500
The film's development began shortly after the release of Final Destination 2; Jeffrey Reddick,
creator of the franchise and a co-writer of the first two films, did not return.

18
00:51:45,000 --> 00:51:51,000
Unlike the second film, which was a direct sequel to the first, the
producers envisioned Final Destination 3 as a standalone film.

19
00:54:37,500 --> 00:54:43,500
The idea of featuring a roller coaster derailment as the opening-scene
disaster came from New Line Cinema executive Richard Bryner.

20
00:57:30,000 --> 00:57:36,000
From the beginning, Wong and Morgan saw control as a major theme in the film.

21
01:00:22,500 --> 01:00:28,500
Casting began in March 2005 and concluded in April.

22
01:03:15,000 --> 01:03:21,000
Like the previous two installments, it was filmed in Vancouver, Canada.

23
01:06:07,500 --> 01:06:13,500
The first two weeks of the three-month shoot were spent filming
the scenes involving the roller coaster derailing.

24
01:09:00,000 --> 01:09:06,000
Following its premiere at Grauman's Chinese Theatre on February 2, 2006, the film
was released in cinemas in the United States on February 10, 2006.

25
01:11:52,500 --> 01:11:58,500
The DVD, released on July 25, 2006, includes commentaries,
documentaries, a deleted scene and an animated video.

26
01:14:45,000 --> 01:14:51,000
A special-edition DVD called "Thrill Ride Edition" includes a feature called "Choose Their Fate", which acts as an interactive
film, allowing viewers to make decisions at specific points in the film that alter the course of the story.

27
01:17:37,500 --> 01:17:43,500
Final Destination 3 received mixed reviews from critics.

28
01:20:30,000 --> 01:20:36,000
The film was a financial success and, with box office receipts of nearly $118
million, the highest-grossing installment in the franchise at the time.

29
01:23:22,500 --> 01:23:28,500
A fourth film, The Final Destination, was released in August 2009.

30
01:26:15,000 --> 01:26:21,000
The film explores themes of dying and death, stroke of
fate, beheading, omen, premonition.

31
01:29:07,500 --> 01:29:13,500
The tone is mind_bending and dark and intense.
